<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allan Ramsay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1684">1684</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Allan Ramsay was a Scottish poet, playwright, publisher and librarian of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, born at Leadhills in Lanarkshire, who worked as a wig-maker before turning bookseller around 1718. His collections The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a>-Table Miscellany (1723–37), of Scots and English songs, and The Ever Green (1724), of Scots poems written before 1600, revived interest in vernacular literature. His pastoral drama The Gentle Shepherd (1725) went through several editions, and he is credited with creating the first circulating library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1726. He is seen as the link between the makars of the 15th and 16th centuries and the later poets Robert Fergusson and Robert Burns. He died in 1758.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allan-ramsay-poet.jpg" alt="Allan Ramsay" width="330" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Aikman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allan_Ramsay,_1684_-_1758.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The title phrase, roughly &quot;old long since&quot; or &quot;days gone by&quot;, also appears in poems by Robert Ayton and <b>Allan Ramsay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li><li><blockquote>Printings were common through the 18th century: <b>Allan Ramsay</b> published &quot;Bonny Barbara Allen&quot; in his Tea-Table Miscellany (1740), and Thomas Percy two versions in his Reliques of Ancient English Poetry (1765).</blockquote><a href="/songs/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a></li></ul></section>
